window.docContents[17].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation--Example___-The-Concrete-Gelfand-Triple-for-Weak-Waves":{"contents":"For the wave equation on a bounded domain , the\nstandard Gelfand triple is\n\n\n\nEach space carries one part of the wave:\n\n*  is the energy space. Poincaré's inequality makes\n an equivalent norm on this space; it measures the\npotential energy stored in spatial gradients. The zero boundary condition is\nencoded by the subscript 0. A wave displacement  has finite\npotential energy.*  is the pivot space. It measures kinetic energy:\n. The total wave energy is\n\nfor the unit-speed Dirichlet wave equation.*  is the dual space. The acceleration \nlives here: it is a distribution of regularity one spatial derivative below\n. This is natural because the wave equation  has\n as a bounded operator.\n\nThe embeddings are dense and continuous, and the pivot space  identifies\nwith its own dual via the Riesz theorem. This identification turns the chain\n\n\n\ninto a Gelfand triple. Lions and Magenes\n(1972, Vol. I, Ch. 1) introduced this framework; Evans (2010, pp. 398–400)\nadapts it to second-order hyperbolic equations.\n\nWith , the abstract identity\n is exactly the weak Dirichlet wave equation\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Weak Waves by Galerkin Approximation","header":"7.3.1.1. Example: The Concrete Gelfand Triple for Weak Waves","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Weak-Waves-by-Galerkin-Approximation--Example___-The-Concrete-Gelfand-Triple-for-Weak-Waves"}});